item.description:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/09/world/europe/france-iran-refrigerators-cyberattack.html&quot;&gt;Interesting&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;U.S. intelligence agencies have recently reached a &amp;#8220;low confidence&amp;#8221; assessment that Iran was responsible for the attacks in France, according to three U.S. officials who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss sensitive intelligence. A &amp;#8220;low confidence&amp;#8221; assessment generally means that there is some intelligence that points in a certain direction but is fragmented, and that the assessment could change as more information is collected.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The agencies&amp;#8217; judgment on the French incidents linked those attacks to Iran generally and not to a specific group such as the Revolutionary Guards, two of the officials said. They added that Iran was also seen as probably responsible for other similar attacks around Europe, but that those investigations were in their early stages...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;hs-featured-image-wrapper&quot;&gt; 
 &lt;a class=&quot;hs-featured-image-link&quot; href=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/the-quantum-leap-preparing-for-post-quantum-cryptography-featuring-verisec&quot; title=&quot;&quot;&gt; &lt;img alt=&quot;The Quantum Leap: Preparing for Post Quantum Cryptography Featuring Verisec&quot; class=&quot;hs-featured-image&quot; src=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/hubfs/2026-blog/verisec-pqc-blog.jpg&quot; style=&quot;width: auto !important; float: left; margin: 0 15px 15px 0;&quot; /&gt; &lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt; 
&lt;br /&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the PCI Security Standards Council’s blog series, The Quantum Leap: Preparing for Post Quantum Cryptography. This special, ongoing feature of our PCI Perspectives blog offers a resource for payment security industry stakeholders to share information about how they are taking steps to ensure their systems are crypto-agile into the future. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.wired.com/story/a-new-ai-tool-from-google-deepmind-formally-verified-the-signal-protocol/&quot;&gt;This&lt;/a&gt; seems like a good thing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Google Deepmind researchers used agentic AI to mathematically prove, or “formally verify,” a number of the Signal protocol’s key cryptographic guarantees­a landmark development for the widely used cryptography and AI itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Conducted as a sort of proof-of-concept project in just four weeks, the work is significant in two ways. It serves as further independent validation of the cryptography that protects massive quantities of the world&amp;#8217;s communications on platforms like the Signal app, Google Messages, and WhatsApp. And it demonstrates Google DeepMind&amp;#8217;s formal verification capabilities using a new AI framework called AlphaProof Code...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
